      "requestAttempts": 1,
      "pronunciationText": "Last time, we left a clue. Pass an electric current through hydrogen gas, and it glows. Now spread that light out with a prism, and you don't get a rainbow. You get just four sharp coloured lines: red, blue-green, blue and violet, with darkness in between. Compare a hot lamp filament. Its light spreads into every colour, with no gaps. So why does hydrogen give out only these few colours? The answer tells us how electrons are arranged in an atom.",
