      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Now, the nuclear model of the atom, from Rutherford's gold foil experiments, has a tiny nucleus with electrons around it. But nothing in that model limits an electron's energy. If an electron could have any energy at all, it could lose any amount, and the atom could give out photons of every energy. That would be a smooth rainbow, like the filament. Hydrogen's lines say that can't be right.",
