        }
      ],
      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's how the light is made. The electric current, or a hot flame, gives an electron energy, and it jumps up to a higher level. We say the atom is excited. But it doesn't stay there. The electron soon drops back to a lower level, and as it drops, it gives out one photon. That photon's energy is exactly the energy gap between the two levels. And an electron sitting in a level gives out no light at all. Light only comes from a jump.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
