      "requestAttempts": 1,
      "pronunciationText": "But Bohr's model had limits. It pictured electrons circling the nucleus in neat orbits, and its sums matched hydrogen's lines very well. For atoms with more than one electron, it didn't work. In nineteen twenty-six, Erwin Schrödinger's model replaced the neat orbits. It keeps the energy levels, but it doesn't track an electron along a path. Instead, it describes orbitals. An orbital is a region around the nucleus where the electron is likely to be found. Picture a cloud that's densest where the electron is most likely to be. Inside the energy levels, orbitals come in types called s, p, d and f, which we'll use in a later video.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
